You take a chance of losing a luck point when you kill a friendly monster
- well worth it if you are tinning tengus.  Your strategy can be improved
a bit.  Since you are dragging the tinning kit around, kill and tin nymphs
and leprechauns.  When you have several tins of tengu meat, then begin
by eating the nymph/leprechaun tins to get the teleport intrinsic; then
eat the tengu tins for teleport control - this improves your odds.

General Comments about Tinning Kits

  1) They are nice early if you are trying to get the Poison Resistance
     intrinisic; once I have a unicorn horn, I don't need the tinning
     kit for that.

  2) Their best use is described above, for gaining teleport control

  3) If a regenerating troll annoys you, you can tin him

  4) Tinning a chameleon corpse gives you the ability to polymorph (once)
     at your convenience - useful if you don't have a wand of polymorph.
     [Obviously, a ring of polymorph control is needed!]

  5) On occasion, you may be forced to do in a monster that could give
     you a nice intrinsic, but you're currently satiated.  If you have
     teleportation and control, just keep teleporting over the corpse.
     If not, the tinning kit can save the corpse for later consumption

  6) It doesn't do any good, but I must admit I derive tremendous
     psychological satisfaction from tinning the Wizard of Yendor :-)

Is all of the above worth the weight of dragging the kit around ?  My
answer is "It depends" -- usually the game situation (including my
strength, owning a bag of holding, etc.) will dictate.

